// Memory: primitive and non-primitive values, copying and sharing, lifetime, clone, mutability
// How memory behaves in assignment, function calls, collections, structs and loops
// Pointers, reference counting and cycles, manual memory, sizes and limits
//
// Raga manages memory for you. What you control is whether a name holds its own value or shares one,
// and when you make a copy. Heap values are reference counted (section 8) and freed the moment nothing refers to them.
// Pointers (section 7) and manual memory (section 9) exist for special cases, and are never needed for ordinary code.


// ============================================================
// 1. TWO KINDS OF VALUES
// ============================================================

// A primitive value lives on the stack. Its name holds the value itself.
//   i8..u128, isize, usize, f32, f64, f128, bool, char, ptr<T>
// A non-primitive value lives on the heap. Its name holds only a reference to it.
//   String, Array, List, Tuple, Set, Map, Optional, Result, and every struct, enum, union and class object
//
// The capital letter of a type name says which one it is: lowercase is a primitive, a capital letter is a
// non-primitive (01-basics section 10). A struct, an enum member or a class object is shared like a List is.

const count = 5                           // the name holds 5
const text = "hello"              // the name holds a reference, the text lives on the heap
// A struct, an enum member or a class object works the same way: the name holds a reference, see section 2.


// ============================================================
// 2. ASSIGNMENT: COPY OR SHARE
// ============================================================

// Assigning a primitive copies it. The two names are independent.
const a = 10
let b = a                                 // b is its own copy of 10
b = 20                                    // a is still 10

// Assigning a non-primitive value copies only the reference. Both names refer to the same value.
const left = [1, 2, 3]
const right = left                        // one List, two names
right.push(4)                             // left sees it too: 1, 2, 3, 4
const alias = left == right               // true: == on a non-primitive value asks if it is the same value

// A struct is a non-primitive value too, so assigning it shares it. There is one Box and two names.
const Box = struct {
    i32 size
    List<i32> items
}
const box1 = Box(size = 1, items = [1, 2])
let box2 = box1                           // not a copy: box2 and box1 refer to the same Box
box2.size = 9                             // box1.size is 9 too
box2.items.push(3)                        // and box1.items has the 3 as well

// Rebinding a name never changes the value the old name refers to.
let list1 = [1]
const list2 = list1
list1 = [2]                           // list1 now refers to a new List, list2 still has 1


// ============================================================
// 3. CLONE: A SEPARATE COPY ON PURPOSE
// ============================================================

// Every non-primitive type has a clone method, and so has every struct. clone makes a separate value.
const original = [1, 2, 3]
const copy = original.clone()
copy.push(4)                              // original is unchanged
const same_value = original == copy        // false: they are two values

// clone copies one level. The new collection is separate, but when its elements are non-primitive values
// they are still the same elements as in the original.
const nested = [[1], [2]]
const shallow = nested.clone()            // a new outer List holding the same two inner Lists
shallow.get(0).push(9)                    // the inner List is shared: nested.get(0) is now 1, 9

// To copy every level, clone the inner values as well.
const deep = List<List<i32>>()
for (nested) |inner| {
    deep.push(inner.clone())
}
deep.get(0).push(5)                       // nested is unchanged

// A struct clone copies its primitive fields and clones its non-primitive fields, so the copy shares nothing.
const box3 = box1.clone()
box3.items.push(7)                        // box1.items is unchanged

// String is immutable, so sharing one is always safe and there is nothing to change.
const name1 = "ann"
const name2 = name1                       // safe: no one can change the text in place
const name3 = name1.upper()               // upper makes a new String, name1 is unchanged


// ============================================================
// 4. CONST, LET AND MUTABLE VALUES
// ============================================================

// const protects the name: it cannot refer to another value, and a primitive cannot change.
// It does not protect a non-primitive value, which can still be changed through any name that refers to it.
const fixed_list = [1, 2]
fixed_list.push(3)                         // allowed: the List changes, the name still refers to it
// fixed_list = [9]                    // ERROR: a const name cannot be rebound

const fixed_box = Box(size = 1, items = List<i32>())
fixed_box.size = 2                         // allowed: const protects the name fixed_box, the Box can change
fixed_box.items.push(1)                    // allowed too
// fixed_box = Box(size = 0, items = List<i32>())   // ERROR: a const name cannot be rebound

// What can change in place:
//   String   nothing, it is immutable
//   Array    an element can be replaced with set, the size is fixed
//   Tuple    a slot can be replaced with set, the size is fixed
//   List     push, pop, shift, unshift, set
//   Set      add, remove
//   Map      set, remove
//   struct   its fields

// To keep a value from changing, share a clone or an immutable value (a String), not the original.


// ============================================================
// 5. FUNCTIONS AND MEMORY
// ============================================================

// Arguments follow the same two rules. A primitive is copied into the parameter.
// A non-primitive value is shared, so a function can change what its caller passed.
const add_ten = function(List<i32> items) {
    items.push(10)                        // changes the caller's List
}
const numbers = [1, 2]
add_ten(numbers)                           // numbers is now 1, 2, 10
add_ten(numbers.clone())                   // numbers is unchanged, the clone changed

const bumped = function(i32 n) i32 {
    let local = n                         // n is a copy of the caller's number
    local = local + 1
    return(local)
}
let level = 1
const next = bumped(level)                // 2, level is still 1

// A function returns a primitive by copying it, and a non-primitive value by sharing it.
// A non-primitive value created inside a function stays alive after the call, because the caller now refers to it.
const make_list = function() List<i32> {
    const made = [1, 2, 3]
    return(made)
}
const kept = make_list()                   // still valid: kept refers to the List made inside

// A default parameter value is created again on every call, so calls never share it.
const collect = function(i32 item, List<i32> into = List<i32>()) List<i32> {
    into.push(item)
    return(into)
}
const c1 = collect(1)                     // 1
const c2 = collect(2)                     // 2, not 1, 2: each call got a new List
// A struct field default is worked out again for every value, like a parameter default. Each List<i32>() written below
// makes its own List, so the two structs do not share one.
const b1 = Box(size = 0, items = List<i32>())
const b2 = Box(size = 0, items = List<i32>())
b1.items.push(1)                          // b2.items is still empty


// ============================================================
// 6. LIFETIME
// ============================================================

// A primitive lives until the end of the block it was declared in.
{
    const short = 1
    const also_short = Box(size = 2, items = List<i32>())
}
// short is gone here. also_short is a name for a Box: the name is gone, and the Box is freed because nothing else refers to it.

// A non-primitive value lives as long as at least one name, field, element or parameter still refers to it.
// When the last reference is gone, the value is freed right away. This is reference counting, see section 8.
let holder = [1, 2, 3]                // the List lives: holder refers to it
holder = [4]                          // the first List has no references left and is freed

// A name going out of scope drops its reference, the value lives on if anything else refers to it.
const outer_ref = List<i32>()
{
    const inner_ref = [1, 2]
    outer_ref.push(i32(inner_ref.len()))         // the inner List is freed at the end of the block: no one else refers to it
}

// A reference kept inside another value counts too.
const owner = List<List<i32>>()
{
    const member = [1]
    owner.push(member)                    // owner now refers to the List, so it outlives member
}
const survivor = owner.get(0)             // still valid

// Temporary values are freed at the end of the statement that created them.
const length = [1, 2, 3].len()        // the List lived only for this statement

// A loop name is a new name on every pass and is gone at the end of each pass.
for (range(0, 3)) |pass| {
    const made = [pass]               // created and freed on every pass
}


// A variable that a function declared inside the block uses (a closure, 04-functions section 15) is the one exception to
// "a primitive lives until the end of its block". The compiler moves it to the heap, in a small cell that the block and
// every function that uses the variable share. The cell is counted like any other heap value, so the variable lives as
// long as the last function that uses it, also after the block or the function that declared it has ended:
const make_total = function() Function<[i32], i32> {
    let total = 0                         // a primitive, but the function below uses it
    const add = function(i32 amount) i32 {
        total = total + amount
        return(total)
    }
    return(add)                            // total is still alive, it now belongs to add
}
const my_total = make_total()
const t1 = my_total(5)                    // 5
const t2 = my_total(7)                    // 12
// Nothing in the code shows the move. A variable that no function uses stays on the stack and costs nothing.
// &total of such a variable is a pointer to the cell, and it is valid as long as the cell lives.


// ============================================================
// 7. POINTERS
// ============================================================

// A pointer holds the address of a variable. Its type is ptr<T>, with the type of what it points to in angle brackets,
// like every type argument. ptr is a primitive type, so its name is lowercase and it is a stack value: copying a pointer copies the address.
// The two operations are:
//   &variable      the address of a variable, a ptr<T>     (prefix &, as in all three)
//   pointer.*      the value the pointer points to, a T    (postfix .*)
// The dereference is written after the pointer, like a field access, so it chains left to right
// and cannot be mistaken for a multiplication: p.* reads "the value of p".
i32 target = 7
ptr<i32> p = &target                      // explicit pointer type
const q = &target                         // inferred: ptr<i32>
const seven = p.*                         // 7
p.* = 8                                   // target is now 8, p.* can be assigned like a variable
p.* += 1                                  // target is now 9, the compound operators work too
const nine = q.*                          // 9, q points to the same variable
// p.* = 2.5                              // ERROR: the pointer is a ptr<i32>, 2.5 is an f64
// ptr<f64> bad = &target                 // ERROR: &target is a ptr<i32>
// const bad = &(1 + 2)                   // ERROR: only a variable has an address, not a temporary value

// & before a name is the address-of operator. Between two values it is still the bitwise and:
//   const mask = flags & 1               // bitwise and
//   const where = &flags                 // address-of

// A pointer must be initialized like any variable. null is the empty pointer, like C's NULL.
// It can be given to any ptr<T>, and only to a ptr<T>: null is not a value of any other type.
ptr<i32> empty = null
const is_empty = empty == null             // true
// i32 count = null                       // ERROR: only a ptr<T> can be null, use Optional<T> for a value that may be absent
// const bad = null                       // ERROR: null has no type of its own, write ptr<i32> p = null
// empty.*                                // ERROR at run time: a null pointer cannot be dereferenced, it throws an Error (06-errors)

// == and != on pointers compare addresses, like == on any value compares what the name holds.
const same = p == q                       // true: both hold the address of target
i32 other = 9
const different = p == &other             // false: another variable, even with the same value

// A pointer to a variable that holds a struct points at the variable, as for any non-primitive value (below).
// A field is read and written through the pointer with a dot, and .* gives the Spot the variable refers to.
const Spot = struct {
    f64 x
    f64 y
}
let spot = Spot(x = 1.0, y = 2.0)
const sp = &spot                          // ptr<Spot>
sp.x = 5.0                                // changes spot.x, no .* needed for a field
const sx = sp.x                           // 5.0
sp.* = Spot(x = 9.0, y = 0.0)             // spot now refers to a new Spot
const spot_copy = sp.*.clone()             // a separate Spot, changing spot_copy leaves spot alone
// sp points at the variable spot. To give C the address of the fields of the Spot, use spot.raw():
// every struct and every untagged union has the layout of a C struct (section 10), and two methods are generated for it,
// like clone:
//   raw()           a ptr<u8>, C's void *: the address of the fields, valid while the struct is alive
//   Type.fromRaw(p) a new struct made by copying the fields from such an address
const raw_spot = spot.raw()                // ptr<u8>: where the fields of spot are, in C layout, to give to a C function
const from_c = Spot.fromRaw(raw_spot)      // a new Spot copied from those bytes, a separate value
// A C function that takes a struct by value gets the fields copied, as the C calling convention says.
// raw() is not &spot: &spot is a pointer to the variable spot, raw() is the address of the fields.

// A function that takes a pointer can change a variable of its caller, which a plain parameter cannot (section 5).
const increment = function(ptr<i32> counter) {
    counter.* = counter.* + 1
}
let hits = 0
increment(&hits)
increment(&hits)                          // hits is 2

// A pointer can point to a pointer. Each .* peels off one level.
ptr<ptr<i32>> pp = &p
const via_two = pp.*.*                     // 9, the value of target

// A pointer to a variable that holds a non-primitive value points at the variable, not at the non-primitive value.
// Assigning through it changes which value the variable refers to.
let words = ["a"]
ptr<List<String>> wp = &words
wp.* = ["b"]                  // words now refers to a new List
wp.*.push("c")                    // .* gives the List, which is then changed in place

// Rules:
//   - There is no pointer arithmetic and no indexing through a pointer: p + 1 and p[0] are errors.
//   - A pointer to a const variable cannot be made: &const_name is an error, because the value must not change.
//   - A pointer does not keep anything alive. It is not a reference, a non-primitive value is freed when nothing else refers to it.
//   - A pointer can be converted only by an explicit cast, like every other conversion:
//       ptr<u8>(p)      from a ptr<i32> to a ptr<u8>, which is C's void *
//       usize(p)        the address as a number
//       ptr<i32>(n)     a number as an address, never checked
// const bad = p + 1                      // ERROR: no pointer arithmetic
// const fixed = 5
// const bad = &fixed                     // ERROR: fixed is const

// A pointer to a variable is only valid while that variable is alive. After its block ends the variable is gone
// and the pointer dangles. Following Raga's rule for C, using a dangling pointer is undefined, it is not checked.
// The obvious case is rejected at compile time:
// const leak = function() ptr<i32> {
//     i32 local = 1
//     return(&local)                      // ERROR: the address of a local variable cannot be returned
// }
// Storing the address somewhere that outlives the block is not checked and is your responsibility:
ptr<i32> outer = null
{
    i32 inner = 5
    outer = &inner                        // outer now holds an address that is valid only inside this block
}
// outer.*                                // undefined: inner is gone

// C types: a C pointer is a ptr<T> of the matching type, and a void * is a ptr<u8>.
//   int *          ptr<i32>        char *         ptr<i8>        void *         ptr<u8>


// ============================================================
// 8. REFERENCE COUNTING AND CYCLES
// ============================================================

// Every non-primitive value keeps a count of the references to it: names, fields, elements and parameters.
// The count goes up when a reference is made (an assignment, a push, a call) and down when one goes away
// (a name leaves its block, a name is rebound, an element is removed).
// When the count reaches zero the value is freed at that very point of the program, not at some later time.
let ref1 = [1, 2]                     // count 1
const ref2 = ref1                         // count 2
const holder2 = List<List<i32>>()
holder2.push(ref1)                        // count 3, the element is a reference too
ref1 = [3]                            // the first List drops to 2, ref1 now refers to a new List
holder2.pop()                             // drops to 1, only ref2 still refers to it
// when ref2 leaves its block the count reaches 0 and the List is freed

// Because values are freed as soon as the last reference goes, there is no waiting and no pause.
// A non-primitive value is freed, and its elements are released with it, so a List of Lists frees all of them.

// A problem comes from cycles: values that refer to each other keep each other's count above zero,
// so none of them is ever freed. That is a memory leak. It is not an error and nothing reports it.
// A cycle is easy to make by accident: a team list that holds members that refer back to the team list.
//
//   const Member = struct {
//       String name
//       List<Member> team                 // a strong reference to the team list
//   }
//   const team = List<Member>()
//   team.push(Member(name = "ann", team = team))    // the list holds a Member that refers to the list
//   // the count of team never reaches 0: the list is leaked
//
// The fix is a weak reference. Weak<T> refers to a non-primitive value without counting: it does not keep it alive.
const Member = struct {
    String name
    Weak<List<Member>> team               // weak: does not keep the team alive
}
const squad = List<Member>()
squad.push(Member(name = "ann", team = Weak(squad)))    // squad is not counted by the member
// when squad leaves its block the count reaches 0, the List is freed and the Member with it

// Weak<T> has two methods. get gives an Optional<T>: Some while the value is alive, None after it was freed.
// alive tells the same as a bool.
const first = squad.get(0)
match (first.team.get()) {
    case (Some) |s| { print(s.value.len()) }      // the team is still alive here, s.value is a strong reference for this case
    case (None)     { print("team is gone") }
}
const still_there = first.team.alive()     // true while squad exists

// Rules for Weak:
//   - It can only refer to a non-primitive value (String, a collection, a struct, a class object). Weak(5) is an error.
//   - It is a non-primitive value that holds the weak handle. Cloning a struct with a Weak field copies the handle, not its target.
//   - get gives a strong reference only inside the case that matched it, so the value cannot be freed while you use it.
// const bad = Weak(5)                    // ERROR: a primitive is not a non-primitive value, nothing to refer to weakly

// Rule of thumb for structures with parents and children: the owner refers to its parts strongly,
// and a part refers back to its owner weakly. Then nothing forms a cycle of strong references.


// ============================================================
// 9. MANUAL MEMORY
// ============================================================

// Almost all code never needs this: lists, strings and the rest manage themselves. Manual memory is for
// the rare case that needs memory with no reference counting at all.
// It is made of three functions, and the memory it gives is not counted and never freed by Raga:
//   alloc<T>(count)   allocates room for count values of the primitive type T, filled with zeros, and gives a ptr<T>
//   p.at(i)           the pointer to element i of what p points to, without any check of the position
//   free(p)           gives the memory back
// T must be a primitive type: non-primitive values already manage themselves.
// There is no pointer arithmetic, p.at(i) is how you reach the other elements.

const buffer = alloc<u8>(4)               // four zeroed bytes, a ptr<u8>
buffer.at(0).* = 65
buffer.at(1).* = 66
const second = buffer.at(1).*             // 66
free(buffer)                              // the memory is released

// One primitive value, on the memory that is managed by hand.
const manual = alloc<i64>()               // count defaults to 1, a ptr<i64> that holds 0
manual.* = 3
free(manual)

// alloc fails by throwing an Error when there is no memory left, see 06-errors.
// Use try / finally so the memory is freed on every path:
const block = alloc<i32>(100)
try {
    block.at(0).* = 1
} finally {
    free(block)
}

// Rules:
//   - Memory from alloc is freed only by free, exactly once. If it is never freed it is a leak.
//   - free(null) does nothing, like in C.
//   - Freeing twice, or using a pointer after free, is undefined. Nothing is checked, as for any dangling pointer.
//   - Only memory from alloc can be freed. free(&x) of a variable is an error, a pointer from & is not allocated.
//   - p.at(i) does not check i, going past the end is undefined, like indexing past the end in C.
//   - alloc and free are not for non-primitive types: alloc<List<i32>>() is an error.
// free(&target)                          // ERROR: target is a variable, it was not allocated
// const bad = alloc<List<i32>>()         // ERROR: a List manages itself


// ============================================================
// 10. SIZES AND LIMITS
// ============================================================

// sizeof<T>() gives the size of a type in bytes as a usize, and alignof<T>() its alignment.
// A primitive has a fixed size known at compile time:
//   i8 1, i16 2, i32 4, i64 8, i128 16, f32 4, f64 8, bool 1, char 4, isize and usize and ptr<T> as wide as a pointer
//   an enum is the size of an i32
//   a struct follows the C layout: the fields in the order they are written, each at a multiple of its alignment,
//   and the whole struct padded to a multiple of its largest alignment
//   a tagged union is as large as its biggest variant plus the tag that says which variant it holds
// For a struct, an enum or a union, sizeof is the size of the object on the heap. The variable that holds it holds a
// reference as wide as a pointer. Every struct and untagged union has this C layout, so raw() can give C its address. String and the collections grow, so they have no fixed size and no sizeof.
const size_of_i32 = sizeof<i32>()           // 4
const size_of_spot = sizeof<Spot>()         // 16: two f64

// Padding: the fields are never reordered, so their order changes the size.
const Loose = struct {
    u8 flag
    i32 count
    u8 mark
}
const Tight = struct {
    i32 count
    u8 flag
    u8 mark
}
const loose_size = sizeof<Loose>()         // 12: 1 + 3 padding + 4 + 1 + 3 padding
const tight_size = sizeof<Tight>()         // 8: 4 + 1 + 1 + 2 padding
const loose_align = alignof<Loose>()       // 4

// Limits:
//   - The stack has a fixed size, set by the system (usually a few megabytes). A function that calls itself
//     without ever stopping overflows it, and the program ends with an error message. That cannot be caught.
//   - The heap is limited by the memory of the machine. Running out of it, in alloc or when a collection grows,
//     throws an Error that can be caught, see 06-errors.
//   - The integer types have fixed sizes, so a value that does not fit is truncated by a cast and never grows,
//     see 01-basics.
// const forever = function(i32 n) i32 { return(forever(n + 1)) }   // runs until the stack is full


// ============================================================
// 11. A VIEW INTO AN ARRAY: A SLICE STRUCT
// ============================================================

// A slice is a part of an Array or a List that is looked at in place, without copying it. It is not a built-in type:
// it is a struct that holds the Array and the part it shows. Because an Array is a non-primitive value, the struct holds a
// reference to the same Array, so the slice and the Array always agree.
const Slice = struct {
    Array<i32> source
    usize start
    usize length

    const get = function(usize index) i32 {
        return(this.source.get(this.start + index))
    }
    const len = function() usize {
        return(this.length)
    }
}

const samples = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50}
const middle = Slice(source = samples, start = 1, length = 3)     // 20, 30, 40
const middle_first = middle.get(0)                 // 20
const middle_len = middle.len()                    // 3
samples.set(1, 99)
const middle_changed = middle.get(0)               // 99: the slice shares the Array, it holds no copy of the samples

// Rules:
//   - A slice is a struct, so it follows every rule of a struct, and it keeps its Array alive (section 8).
//   - To get a separate part instead, copy the samples into a new Array or List: the slice and the copy then do not agree.
//   - Nothing checks the part against the Array: get with an index outside the part reads what the Array has there, or throws
//     when the Array has no such position.


// ============================================================
// 12. COMMON MISTAKES
// ============================================================

// Each mistake is shown with what happens, and with the way that does what was meant.

// 1. Expecting a copy when you get a shared value.
const cm_items = [1, 2]
const cm_not_backup = cm_items                    // not a backup, the same List
const cm_backup = cm_items.clone()                // a backup
cm_items.push(3)
const cm_backup_len = cm_backup.len()             // 2: the clone did not change
const cm_shared_len = cm_not_backup.len()         // 3: it is the same List as cm_items

// 2. Changing a List while looping over it. It throws an Error (03-control-flow section 10), so loop over a clone.
const cm_queue = [1, 2, 3]
for (cm_queue.clone()) |cm_n| {
    if (cm_n == 2) { cm_queue.push(9) }           // fine: the loop goes over the clone, not over cm_queue
}
// for (cm_queue) |cm_n| { cm_queue.push(cm_n) }     // throws an Error: the List changes while the loop goes over it

// 3. Putting the same List in two places and changing one of them.
const cm_row = [0, 0]
const cm_grid_bad = [cm_row, cm_row]              // two entries, one List
cm_grid_bad.get(0).set(0, 5)
const cm_both_changed = cm_grid_bad.get(1).get(0) // 5: changing one entry changed the other
const cm_grid_ok = [cm_row.clone(), cm_row.clone()]   // two separate rows

// 4. Comparing with == and expecting equal contents. == on a non-primitive value asks if it is the same value.
const cm_left = [1]
const cm_right = [1]
const cm_equal = cm_left == cm_right              // false: two Lists
const cm_parts_equal = cm_left.get(0) == cm_right.get(0)    // true: compare the parts yourself

// 5. Relying on a struct copy to protect a List inside it. The new struct shares the List, clone the List too.
const cm_box = Box(size = 1, items = [7])
const cm_second_bad = Box(size = cm_box.size, items = cm_box.items)           // the same List inside
const cm_second_ok = Box(size = cm_box.size, items = cm_box.items.clone())    // its own List
cm_box.items.push(8)
const cm_bad_len = cm_second_bad.items.len()      // 2: it saw the change
const cm_ok_len = cm_second_ok.items.len()        // 1: it did not

// 6. Keeping a pointer to a variable that has gone out of scope, see section 7.
//      returning &local from a function is a compile error, and a pointer stored elsewhere is not checked.
// 7. Expecting an assignment through a pointer to a heap variable to change the List itself: it changes the variable's reference.
//      p.* = [9] makes the variable refer to a new List, the old List is not touched.
// 8. Making a cycle of strong references and expecting it to be freed, see section 8. Make the way back a Weak.

// 9. Forgetting free on memory from alloc, or calling it twice, see section 9. Use try / finally.
const cm_block = alloc<i32>(4)
try {
    cm_block.at(1).* = 7
} finally {
    free(cm_block)                                // once, whatever happened inside the try
}

// 10. Expecting sizeof of a struct to include what its heap fields hold: it is the size of the struct itself, and a field that
//     holds a List or a String is only one reference. The number of elements does not change it.

// 11. Assuming a struct is as small as its fields add up: padding is added, see section 10.
const PaddedPair = struct {
    u8 flag
    u32 count
}
const cm_padded_size = sizeof<PaddedPair>()       // 8, not 5: three bytes of padding come after flag
